WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:14.004
BRIDGE TROLL · SOLUTION
Two soldering faults, two fixes
Diagnosis (see "How to approach"): a solder bridge shorts D2, and a cold joint on R1 opens D1's branch.
Fix: wick the bridge, reflow the cold joint, check both LEDs, then verify on the test bench.
Tools: Desolder wick (4) · Soldering iron (3) · Multimeter (2) · Select (1)

2
00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:34.731
STEP 1 · REMOVE THE BRIDGE
Desolder wick (4): click the bridge between D2's legs.
Why: Wick is braided copper soaked in flux. Heated on the blob, it pulls molten solder into the braid by capillary action and leaves the two pads separate.

3
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:41.245
STEP 1 · CHECK
Power off, diode test again: D2 now reads 2.105 V, a healthy LED.

4
00:00:41.245 --> 00:00:58.897
STEP 2 · REFLOW
Soldering iron (3): click R1's cold joint to reflow it.
Why: Reheating with fresh flux lets the solder melt fully and wet both pad and lead. A good joint ends up shiny with a concave fillet.

5
00:00:58.898 --> 00:01:12.092
STEP 3 · CHECK
Power on, press SW1: both LEDs light and the supply shows 14.5 mA.
Why: Each branch carries (3 V − LED voltage) / 100 Ω. Both LEDs drop about 2 V, and the coin cell's own ~8 Ω internal resistance costs a little more: about 8 mA each, ~16 mA together.

6
00:01:12.093 --> 00:01:27.528
STEP 4 · VERIFY
Run the test bench (Run tests).
Why: It powers the board, checks every objective, and only decrypts the flag if the circuit now matches the original design: a fix that merely works around a fault does not count.

7
00:01:27.529 --> 00:01:31.131
STEP 4 · VERIFY
All objectives pass and the flag is captured. Earn your own: it is blurred here.

8
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:45.134
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Rework, then re-measure
Wick removes excess solder; the iron with flux reflows a cold joint.
Re-test the part you fixed: the same diode test that found the short proves it is gone.
Predict the current with I = (V − V_LED) / R and compare it with the supply.
